abstract: >-
  Post-training quantization and mixed-precision search commonly assume that
  layer-wise quantization sensitivity is an intrinsic property of the
  architecture and weight distribution, so that a single representative
  calibration set suffices to decide per-layer bit allocation. This work tests
  that assumption for signal-classification models under distribution shift.
  We define rho as the Spearman rank correlation between per-layer quantization
  sensitivities measured in a source domain and in a shifted target domain,
  and we complement it with an equal-reconstruction-error arm that quantifies
  the cost of not knowing the deployment domain. On two architecturally
  different backbones (CLDNN, 10 layers; 1D-ResNet, 16 layers) over five
  target domains including a null-shift control, the cost of domain ignorance
  is zero on the control and consistently positive under real shift, reaching
  166 percent relative damage. We further show that rho is not a reliable
  carrier for the conclusion: it depends strongly on the chosen reference
  bit-width and on the backbone, with one domain pair flipping sign across
  backbones.
